## Resource Naming

By default, this chart uses release-name based resource naming for historical reasons and
backwards compatibility with existing deployments.

> [!WARNING]
> For new deployments, it is recommended to set `useLegacyNaming: false` to use operator-style
> names (e.g. `vmanomaly-<release>`). This aligns resource names with the
> [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) convention and
> simplifies future migration to operator-managed resources.

### VictoriaMetrics operator

With `operator.enabled: true` the chart renders a [VMAnomaly](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vmanomaly/) resource
instead of its own Deployment/StatefulSet, Service, config ConfigMap, PodDisruptionBudget and VMPodScrape,
and the [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) manages the workload.
The operator must already be installed in the cluster. Operator mode always uses operator-style names, regardless of `useLegacyNaming`.

The VMAnomaly is named after the release (or `fullnameOverride`), so the operator creates `vmanomaly-<release>` objects.
Ingress and HTTPRoute are still rendered by the chart and target the `vmanomaly-<release>` Service, which the operator always creates as a headless Service on the server port.

Settings without an operator equivalent fail rendering, with a message pointing to the replacement:

- `replicationFactor`, which the VMAnomaly doesn't support;
- `config.reader` and `config.writer` settings without a typed field, including inline credentials; set `operator.spec.reader` and `operator.spec.writer` instead;
- `config.monitoring.push`; set `operator.spec.monitoring.push` instead;
- `config.server` settings other than `addr`, `port`, `path_prefix`, `max_concurrent_tasks`, `ui_default_state` and `use_reader_connection_settings`; set `operator.spec.server` instead;
- Service settings the operator can't apply, since it always creates a headless `ClusterIP` Service on the server port: `service.type`, node port, load balancer, external IPs, traffic and IP family settings, labels and annotations, and a `service.servicePort` different from the server port;
- `podMonitor` `relabelings`, `metricRelabelings`, `port`, `targetPort`, `path`, `interval`, `scrapeTimeout`, `scheme`, `basicAuth` and `tlsConfig`; set `operator.spec.serviceScrapeSpec` instead;
- `networkPolicy` labels, annotations and `extraLabels`, since the operator-managed NetworkPolicy has no custom metadata.

Fields that have no chart value, or need a different value, go to `operator.spec`, which is deep-merged over the generated spec:

```yaml
operator:
  enabled: true
  spec:
    logLevel: WARN
```

Probes keep the operator defaults. Switching a release to `operator.enabled: true` recreates its pods.

